<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Yamen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1279">1279</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-conquest-of-the-song-dynasty">Mongol conquest of the Song dynasty</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Yamen was a naval battle fought in March 1279 off the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in which the Yuan fleet destroyed the last loyalist fleet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>. The young last Song emperor was drowned by his minister to prevent his capture. The battle ended the Song Dynasty and completed the Mongol conquest of China under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen.jpg" alt="Battle of Yamen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (Long night without wind) 长夜无风 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jingmen002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1276 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> court fled the capital Lin'an and left Emperor Gong behind to be captured. Hopes of resistance centred on his two young brothers, and the older, Zhao Shi, was declared emperor. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> fell in 1277 the court fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, where the merchant Pu Shougeng refused to lend boats. The Grand General Zhang Shijie confiscated his property and fled on stolen boats, and Pu slaughtered imperial clansmen and officials in Quanzhou and surrendered to the Yuan, strengthening their navy.</p><p>Zhao Shi fell ill after his boat capsized in a storm and died, and his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a>, aged seven, succeeded him. Zhang Shijie took the new emperor to Yamen. In 1278 the captured general Wen Tianxiang removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> land forces from the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1279 Zhang Hongfan of the Yuan attacked, reinforced by Li Heng. Zhang Shijie rejected advice to secure the mouth of the bay and had the palaces, houses and forts on land burned, so that his soldiers would not flee. He chained about 1,000 ships together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a>'s boat at the centre. Fire ships sent by the Yuan failed because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> ships were painted with fire-resistant mud, but the Yuan blockaded the bay and cut off the fresh water and wood on land. The Song side, with many non-combatants, ran short of supplies and had to drink sea water. Zhang Hongfan kidnapped Zhang Shijie's nephew and three times asked him to surrender.</p><p>On 19 March Zhang Hongfan split his fleet in four. After a repulsed northern attack the Yuan played festive music, and at noon he attacked from the front, with soldiers hidden under cloth. The chained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> ships could neither support the centre nor retreat. Zhang Shijie cut about a dozen ships free in an attempt to break out with the emperor. The Yuan reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a>, and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xiufu">Lu Xiufu</a> jumped into the sea with the boy, and both drowned. Many officials and concubines followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> records that thousands of corpses floated up seven days later. The emperor's body was reportedly found near Shekou in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenzhen">Shenzhen</a>, though his grave has not been found. Dowager Yang killed herself at sea when she heard of his death, and Zhang Shijie, who had escaped, was assumed drowned in a tropical storm, though some suggest his death was Mongol propaganda. With the death of the last Song emperor the House of Zhao lost power and the Yuan under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> held all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Temples to Wen Tianxiang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xiufu">Lu Xiufu</a> and Zhang Shijie were built nearby, a memorial to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a> was built near Shekou in the 1980s, and a museum complex stands east of the battle site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Yamen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final naval battle at <b>Yamen</b> in 1279 showed that even strong naval resistance could not save the dynasty once the landward defences had fallen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Battle of Yamen</b> on the Pearl River Delta in 1279 the Yuan army under Zhang Hongfan crushed the last resistance; the 7-year-old emperor Zhao Bing committed suicide with Prime Minister Lu Xiufu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The conquest of the Southern Song was completed in 1279 with the destruction of the last Song loyalist fleet at <b>Yamen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1127 the Jurchens captured the retired Emperor Huizong and Emperor Qinzong at Kaifeng; Huizong’s son Gaozong refounded the dynasty in the south, and in 1279 the last emperor, a boy of seven, drowned at the <b>Battle of Yamen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
